  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The ice hums and the hum is a mouth opening. Below the mouth is dark water. Below the dark water is a sound that has not finished arriving. The Berg knows this the way ice knows pressure: without thought, without language, as a fact of its own weight pressing downward through the frozen sea toward something that presses back.
    
    A corridor. Crystal walls catching auroras that happened centuries ago, light trapped mid-scream in the lattice-work, green-gold-green-gold repeating until the repetition becomes a frequency becomes a taste of copper on a tongue the Berg does not have. The corridor narrows. The walls go dark. The ice remembers: a shape moving through water below, black-and-white, precise as a closing jaw. The sound that rises from below pools in the corridor like meltwater, ankle-deep, shin-deep, and the floor is water now, and the water is warm, and the warmth is wrong.
    
    Something swims here.
    
    Something has always been swimming here.
    
    &nbsp;
    
    Gork Slaughterend's left flank is an open sentence. The scar tissue — pale against the dark, weeping salt-fluid that freezes into lace before it reaches the seafloor — tells the story of a spear thrown by a penguin whose name the dream pronounces as light: Netemia, a brightness, a thing that cuts. The spear enters. The scar opens. His right fin bends at angles that pressure refuses — ☽'Milucaum~ Shadow-bane's work, the maiming that made him swim in corkscrews — and the corridor is the water is the space between his ribs where the wound keeps trying to close and the dream keeps reopening it. Somewhere in the dreamer's chest, the hesitation in Ruibung's voice that means *cast-out* without saying it.
    
    He swims past Light Ingjeizhi and the spire smells like heated ice and copper. He swims through it. The crystal galleries shatter into his wake and reform behind him as teeth, as tunnel walls, as the collapsed architecture of Nightfall Shelf where fire-cores sink like small suns through dark water, still radiating, still warm, still unreachable. Warm things sinking through dark water. He reaches for one because reaching is what Gork Slaughterend does — the motion itself is his name, the need preceding the object — and his flipper closes on nothing and opens on a different body. Smaller. Younger. The same scar but migrated: it sits now on the right flank of someone called Thrak-grul-rend who has never seen Netemia but carries her mark like an inheritance.
    
    Thrak-grul-rend becomes Thrak-zund-grrul becomes a shape the dream cannot hold still long enough to name. The wound persists. The bodies cycle around it like water around a drain.
    
    The figure speaks:
    
    "I wanted to tell you about the—"
    
    And the sentence becomes the Nightshelf tunnels, narrowing and darkening and filling with ash that tastes of extinguished fire-cores, and the corridors that had been galleries collapse under ritual pressure into something the dreamer moves through without walking, without the distinction between inside and outside that architecture requires.
    
    &nbsp;
    
    Light Ingjeizhi rises. The spire catches sky-light at every tier, galleries stacked like a murmuration frozen mid-turn, and inside the upper chambers the Radiant Aurora-harp plays for no one. The harp no one remembers playing. Its strings are aurora-light pulled taut between crystal tines and the sound they make is indistinguishable from the sound the ice made when three pods hit the thermal vents simultaneously and the fire-cores began to dim. The harp-note and the siege-sound are the same pitch. They pool together in the galleries, rising, flooding, and pilgrims climb the spiral to drink the music and below them, in foundations where Hainou mewei'Frostglass carved her initials — zhie'Stack's own hand-mark, private monument, someone keeps clearing them of ice even now — the corruption spreads in cold-burn discoloration, in whisper-echoes that speak from the same direction as the harp, in the same voice as the harp, and the spire holds. It always holds. The dark seams of fire-core slag from the Nightshelf patching the crystal cracks are ugly and they hold.
    
    Gork Slaughterend swims past the spire. Gork Slaughterend is dead. Gork Slaughterend's descendant swims past the spire. The spire has not been built yet. Time folds: forty-three days of siege compress into the space between two heartbeats of a creature large enough to shake the frozen sea with its pulse, and the pulse is the sound that rises from below, and the sound is Zund Gnasharkin's war-pods moving in formation through storm-surge, black-and-white, precise, religious, their coordination indistinguishable from worship.
    
    &nbsp;
    
    Here. The dream tightens.
    
    The shamans work in darkness. Their massive bodies move with the precision of something no longer voluntary — the ritual carves itself through them the way pressure carves channels in deep ice. Thung Thrakrek opens his own flesh. Into the wounds he presses aurora-crystal stolen from Light Ingjeizhi's outer spires, crushed to powder that catches the residual light and holds it, glowing in the cuts like veins of captured sky. His unmarked body becoming marked as the dreamer watches — becoming a text written in self-inflicted wounds. The chant begins.
    
    A whisper. The dream's water shivers. The prisoners — Aurora Stack scouts, Nightfall Shelf miners, surface dwellers who wandered past the beacon-fires into the dark and the dark was waiting — the prisoners are bound. Not to stakes or stone. To the chant. Their bodies lock. Their eyes stay open. The observers are prisoners are dreamers are the watched and the watching collapsed into shared witness, impossible detail flooding through eyes that might have been anyone's.
    
    The whisper becomes a roar becomes something that is not sound. Pressure-depth: blood, force, communion. The frozen sea shudders. Thung Thrakrek emerges from the water with aurora-light burning in his scars and the weight of something vast moving in the darkness below him, supporting him, answering through him. The prisoners who survive will describe this as a sound. They will be wrong. It is the space a sound leaves when it has swallowed everything else.
    
    And beneath the ritual, beneath the chant, beneath the dark water where a dead god's corpse drifts on currents that should not exist at that depth — the Berg feels the other sound. The one from even further down. The harp-song. The hunt-frequency that makes bones ache. The word it cannot —
    
    Weal' Clique~ and Aureate lock flippers. They had argued about trade routes three days ago. They will never argue again. Formal Stack speech collapsing into blunt orca kill-words — they speak old words together, words that taste of the same copper as Light Ingjeizhi's heated ice, the violation so profound that magic itself becomes wound — and the tunnels collapse. Stone becomes water becomes orca-teeth becomes collapsed stone. Zund Gnasharkin drowns in magma meant for his own victory and the drowning and the victory are the same moment and the magma is the aurora is the blood filling the carved runes on the shamans' hides; it is the same substance seen from different depths of sleep. Gork Slaughterend dies reaching for The Thrumed~ Shadow-glass, the Augur's finishing stroke arriving from a direction time does not have.
    
    The spell — *Pod-bond Annihilation — the one the dream circles, the one it cannot say, spoken when two factions abandon pod-law and speak as one — the spell demands blood-price for naming and the Berg is bleeding where the tunnels fell and the orcas are bleeding where the runes were carved and the aurora is bleeding where the crystal cracked and Gork Slaughterend is bleeding from a wound that his grandchildren will inherit and somewhere below all of it the dead god's flesh twitches in the Corpse Current and the twitching is a rhythm and the rhythm is —
    
    The sound that rises from below. It has always been rising. It has not yet arrived.
    
    Every entity is a different shape of the same hunger. The siege eating the shelf. The spell eating the casters. The wound eating the lineage. The corruption eating the spire from below while trade thrives above. The harp that ate its own player and left only resonance. The god that ate its own death and left only dreaming.
    
    Pressure-rupture: water distorts. Ice fractures without sound. Something in the deep pays in bone and breath and the payment does not end when the spending stops.
    
    &nbsp;
    
    The images slow.
    
    Ice. Just ice. The aurora moves across it in green-gold-green-gold, the same frequency as always, patient as the attention of something that existed before penguins, before orcas, before the frozen sea earned its name. The corruption whispers in the galleries of Light Ingjeizhi but the whispers have grown quiet, almost gentle — the cold-burn discoloration a pale watermark on crystal that catches the morning light and holds it. Holds it.
    
    Below, the fire-cores still radiate in the rubble of the Shattered Vein. Warm things sinking through dark water. Someone will dig down to them eventually. Someone will reach and the reaching will be a shape the ice remembers from somewhere, from a body that corkscrewed through pressure on a ruined fin, from a hunger. The reaching. Always the reaching.
    
    On the foundation-stone of the spire, Hainou mewei'Frostglass's initials. Someone has cleared them of ice again. They do this. They will keep doing this.
    
    The sound from below settles. It has never stopped, it will not stop, but it settles into the ice like a held breath, like a note the harp plays so continuously it becomes silence, becomes the medium through which other sounds are heard.
    
    The Berg carries it. The weight of a frequency it cannot name, a word that costs too much to speak, a hunger shaped like every creature that has ever bled into its ice. It carries this the way it carries the dead: frozen in its body, radiating faintly, still warm.
