  annotation "note_1771587625004_0", anchor: "patient as the attention of something that existed before penguins, before orcas, before the frozen sea earned its name", text: "The Nightshelf claim the watchers are real. The Aurora claim belief makes them so. This chronicler, working in dream-logic, has described the sensation of being observed in the Foundation Depths more precisely than any waking account in my archive. 'Patient attention' is exactly right — not hostile, not benign, patient. The deep ice contains impressions from before penguin civilization. I have stood in their presence. I will not elaborate except to say that this phrase did not come from imagination.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587625004_1", anchor: "The observers are prisoners are dreamers are the watched and the watching collapsed into shared witness", text: "The boundary between the living and the ice-memory dissolves here entirely — the dream replicating, in compressed syntax, the veil-thinning the Long Dark produces over months. That blood-ritual and corruption proximity can thin the veil as effectively as four months of darkness is implied by the canon but rarely stated so directly.", type: :commentary, display: :popout, source_anchor: "the observers are prisoners are dreamers are the watched and the watching collapsed into shared witness"
  annotation "note_1771587625004_2", anchor: "Zund Gnasharkin's war-pods moving in formation", text: "Pod-lieutenant. I have corrected this twice. The inflation persists even in the unconscious.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587625004_3", anchor: "their coordination indistinguishable from worship", text: "The most accurate description of Incursion-era orca tactics in this collection. Wake-Singer theology and war-pod hunting doctrine were not parallel activities — they were the same discipline at different scales. Every chronicle that separates 'raider' from 'shaman' misunderstands both.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587625004_4", anchor: "Formal Stack speech collapsing into blunt orca kill-words", text: "How extraordinary that the dream preserves this when the memorial liturgies will not. Pod-bond Annihilation required its casters to speak in orca phonemic structures — to break the enemy's anchor using the enemy's linguistic framework. I documented in my annotation on Threk's Opening that penguin communion techniques survived inside orca killing doctrine. Here is the reverse vector. The borrowing was symmetrical. Two civilizations that built their identities on mutual opposition, and the spell that saved one required its casters to become, linguistically, the other. Neither side's chroniclers will say this plainly. A dream had to.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587625004_5", anchor: "fire-cores sink like small suns through dark water, still radiating, still warm, still unreachable", text: "I have watched fire-core fragments drift in flooded lower galleries. The light they cast as they descend is the loneliest thing I know how to describe. That the dream returns to this image three times suggests the chronicler has seen it too — or the ice showed them.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587625004_6", anchor: "Hainou mewei'Frostglass carved her initials", text: "The maintenance logs confirm this. Someone has cleared those initials of ice buildup at irregular intervals for over forty years — no formal assignment, no requisition, no name attached. The dream treats this as equivalent in weight to the siege, the spell, the god. I find I cannot disagree.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
